Concern over a stronger Federal authority for regulation of the taking of endangered species appeared most frequently in State comments, particularly from State game and fish agencies. This concern appears to result from a State feeling that the provision would be precident setting and would breech the traditional State responsibility for physical legal protection of fish and wildlife. It should be noted that this precedent has already been set by the Migratory Bird Treaty Acts which place protection of all migratory bird species under Federal authority. Likewise, designated species of marine mammals are brought under Federal protection by the Marine Mammal Act of 1972 and destruction of either species of eagle is a violation of Federal law under authority of the Bald Eagle Act. Placing species of vertebrates, mollusks and crustaceans determined by the Secretary to be endangered under Federal protection would not be precedent setting.
Written comments seem to indicate many States interpret the proposed legislation as relieving the States of all management responsibility for endangered species. Comment from the Bureau of Land Management observes that the draft Environmental Impact Statement does not make clear the new authority of the States under the proposed legislation. The BLM statement goes on to recommend a discussion of the change in authority. As a result, this part of the EIS under <u>Description of the Proposed Action</u> has been revised to more fully explain the impact of the action.
